  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 352 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis shows surface high pressure lingering \nover the eastern half of Lake Superior, supporting areas of locally \ndense fog over the lake and adjacent parts of the eastern UP, mainly \nfrom Luce County eastward. Clear skies have allowed temperatures \ndrop into the 40s across the central UP, with a few of the colder \nspots getting into the upper 30s. Temperatures are holding in the \n50s in the west as insulating high cloud cover begins to stream \nover the region ahead more unsettled weather beginning to take shape \nover the northern Plains.\n\nRAP analysis and water vapor imagery reveal a split flow pattern \nacross the western half of North America, with one shortwave trough \nemerging form the Canadian Rockies while a cutoff low spins over the \nDesert Southwest. Deep southwest flow is becoming established across \nthe central CONUS ahead of this large scale troughing, with a plume \nof rich moisture characterized by 1.5+ inch PWAT values taking aim \non the northern Mississippi River Valley and eventually the Upper \nGreat Lakes. In the meantime, expect another pleasant day across \nUpper Michigan today as morning fog eventually dissipates and gives \nway to temperatures in the low to mid 70s amidst southerly low level \nflow. Could see a few showers develop across the far western UP by \nlater this afternoon into this evening as a weak disturbance grazes \nthe western half of Lake Superior ahead of the trough moving out of \nwestern Canada, with rain chances spreading east across the rest of \nthe UP tonight.\n\nLatest guidance continues to trend more pessimistic with regard to \nrain chances across the UP from Wednesday into Thursday as the \nnorthern and southern stream energy remain out of phase and leave \nthe UP stuck in the middle between systems. The western Canada \ntrough eventually spawns a deep surface low that will track well \nnorth of the area into Hudson Bay on Wednesday. Meanwhile, another \nlow will develop over the South Plains and lift northeast towards \nthe Great Lakes through Thursday, but the bulk of the guidance keeps \nthis feature well south of the area, with the southcentral and \neastern UP seeing the best chances for meaningful rainfall out of \nthis system. As such, NBM probs for a wetting rain of a quarter inch \nhave dwindled to around 25% across the northern tier of the UP \nthrough midweek, while the southcentral UP remains around 75% or \nhigher. Still, chances for more significant rainfall have come down \nacross southcentral, with probs for as much as a half inch now under \n50%.  \n\nCooler west-northwest flow will bring breezy conditions on Thursday \nas near gales on Lake Superior could translate to winds gusting to \n35-40 mph in the Keweenaw and 30+ mph east along the shoreline. \nAdditionally increasing Delta Ts as 850 mb temps plummet to around 0 \nC while lake surface temps remain around 13-16 C could result in \nlake induced rain showers or drizzle along the higher terrain of the \nnorth central UP on Thursday. More tranquil weather returns late in \nthe week as high pressure builds over the northern Plains and shifts \neastward, bringing seasonable temps in the low to mid 60s. Will have \nto keep an eye on frost concerns from Thursday night onward. Another \nsystem looks to bring additional rain chances later in the weekend.",
